WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.340
Reflection can reveal architecture and implementation details that are not meant for broad exposure.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:19.240
Metadata may include assembly names, namespaces, type names, property names, method names, attributes, generic type shapes, internal conventions, feature flags, and dependency details.

3
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.240
That information may not be sensitive in isolation, but context matters.

4
00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:35.940
Internal names and conventions can help unauthorized users understand how the system is organized, which features exist, which components are loaded, and which dependencies or plugins are present.

5
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.760
That can increase the value of other observations.

6
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:50.160
Logs, debug endpoints, API responses, admin tools, plugin inventories, health checks, and exception messages can expose more metadata than intended.

7
00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:58.020
A diagnostic page that is useful during development may be too detailed for production clients or low-trust support workflows.

8
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:03.820
Diagnostics should provide enough information for operators without exposing internal structure broadly.

9
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:12.480
Safe context might include an operation name, a stable type key, a category, a correlation ID, an environment, and a safe version identifier.

10
00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:20.100
Raw internal type lists, assembly paths, and reflected values should be limited to tightly controlled operational contexts.

11
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:28.780
Reflection-based inspection tools should be access-controlled, environment-scoped, redacted, and reviewed before production.

12
00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:35.920
If the tool helps operators understand runtime state, it also needs ownership, logging, and a clear audience.
